WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.040 --> 00:00:05.060
Kong: Skull Island is the second movie in
Legendary and Warner Bros' Godzilla and King

00:00:05.060 --> 00:00:09.040
Kong MonsterVerse, and it features a cool
scene right at the end of all the credits

00:00:09.040 --> 00:00:12.910
with some exciting teases for the Godzilla
sequel, King of the Monsters.

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:16.840
Yippee ki-yay movie lovers, it's Jan here,
and in this video, I want to take a look at

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:20.760
what we can look forward to in Godzilla 2
based on the post-credits scene in Kong: Skull

00:00:20.760 --> 00:00:24.390
Island and I'm also going to talk a bit about
Godzilla vs Kong.

00:00:24.390 --> 00:00:28.430
If you haven't seen Kong: Skull Island yet,
there are spoilers ahead for that film plus

00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.610
the Godzilla movies and Kong movies.

00:00:30.610 --> 00:00:34.870
To avoid those, click here for my spoiler-free
video of the movie and venture back here after

00:00:34.870 --> 00:00:36.430
you've had a chance to see it!

00:00:36.430 --> 00:00:41.010
OK, then, let's get stuck in: After all the
credits have rolled, we see Tom Hiddleston

00:00:41.010 --> 00:00:44.330
and Brie Larson, stuck in an interrogation
room by themselves.

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:48.900
They seem to be talking to some mysterious
unseen figures behind a two-way mirror and

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:52.550
they agree to pretend that Skull Island never
existed and they never went there.

00:00:52.550 --> 00:00:57.290
Corey Hawkins and Tian Jing then walk in and
welcome Hiddleston and Larson to Monarch,

00:00:57.290 --> 00:01:00.480
an organisation that covertly studies monsters
like Godzilla.

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:05.309
The two Monarch staff then explain to Hiddleston
and Larson that Skull Island is just the beginning

00:01:05.309 --> 00:01:07.250
and that there are more monsters out there.

00:01:07.250 --> 00:01:11.690
They warn that the world never belonged to
humans, instead it belonged to the monsters,

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:15.470
and that the question is how long it will
be before the monsters take it back.

00:01:15.470 --> 00:01:19.750
Hawkins then reveals that "Kong is not the
only King" and shows a set of files Monarch

00:01:19.750 --> 00:01:21.390
has on other monsters.

00:01:21.390 --> 00:01:26.470
This is when we get to see some cave paintings
of Godzilla and three iconic kaiju; Rodan,

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:27.830
Mothra and King Ghidorah.

00:01:27.830 --> 00:01:32.020
The final drawing shows Godzilla fighting
the three-headed monster Ghidorah and, as

00:01:32.020 --> 00:01:35.330
the screen fades to black, we hear the iconic
Godzilla roar.

00:01:35.330 --> 00:01:40.590
OK, let's look in more detail at this post-credits
scene and what it means for the new MonsterVerse.

00:01:40.590 --> 00:01:46.200
Rodan is a type of pteranodon that first appeared
on the big screen in Toho Studios' 1956 movie

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.689
Rodan, and was initially a foe of Godzilla's
in the 1964 movie "Ghidorah: The Three-Headed

00:01:51.689 --> 00:01:52.689
Monster".

00:01:52.689 --> 00:01:57.080
Mothra, as the name suggests, is a moth-like
creature that has often been portrayed as

00:01:57.080 --> 00:01:58.080
a saviour.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.909
She's one of movie studio Toho's most popular
kaiju and is surpassed only by Godzilla in

00:02:02.909 --> 00:02:04.979
the number of movies that she's appeared in.

00:02:04.979 --> 00:02:11.530
She went up against Godzilla in the 1964 movie
Mothra vs Godzilla and also in 1992's Godzilla

00:02:11.530 --> 00:02:16.159
vs Mothra, so it's quite possible we'll see
her as an antagonist to Godzilla, at least

00:02:16.159 --> 00:02:18.019
to start with, in Godzilla 2.

00:02:18.019 --> 00:02:23.029
By the way, the 2014 Godzilla movie included
a couple of Mothra easter eggs that already

00:02:23.029 --> 00:02:25.129
teased the iconic kaiju.

00:02:25.129 --> 00:02:29.219
Mothra's name appeared on some labels stuck
together on an old dilapidated tank at Joe

00:02:29.219 --> 00:02:30.409
and Ford's former home.

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:34.769
And there was a very heavy butterfly and moth
theme going on in Ford's class the day the

00:02:34.769 --> 00:02:36.519
nuclear plant went down.

00:02:36.519 --> 00:02:40.409
The paintings of a dragon-like creature in
the end credits are representations of King

00:02:40.409 --> 00:02:41.599
Ghidorah.

00:02:41.599 --> 00:02:45.459
Ghidorah was a powerful enemy of Godzilla,
Mothra and Rodan when he first appeared in

00:02:45.459 --> 00:02:47.180
Ghidorah: The Three-Headed Monster.

00:02:47.180 --> 00:02:51.749
The idea for Ghidorah's multiple heads was
inspired by a Japanese mythological eight-headed

00:02:51.749 --> 00:02:52.749
creature.

00:02:52.749 --> 00:02:57.650
In the 1964 Toho movie, the monstrous dragon
is an extra-terrestrial being that hatches

00:02:57.650 --> 00:03:00.359
from an egg which initially appeared to be
a meteor.

00:03:00.359 --> 00:03:04.669
Ghidorah attempts to take over the Earth until
Mothra stands up to him, with Godzilla and

00:03:04.669 --> 00:03:08.620
Rodan eventually deciding to team up with
her to defeat Ghidorah and send him back to

00:03:08.620 --> 00:03:09.620
space.

00:03:09.620 --> 00:03:14.449
Other appearances of Ghidorah in Toho's movies
have him under the control of aliens or time-travellers,

00:03:14.449 --> 00:03:18.409
and there's one incarnation of him in which
he's a heroic guardian monster that protected

00:03:18.409 --> 00:03:19.409
Japan.

00:03:19.409 --> 00:03:24.680
By the way, the 2014 Godzilla movie included
a little easter egg to Ghidorah via a submerged

00:03:24.680 --> 00:03:28.769
dragon head where the female MUTO had laid
her eggs in San Francisco.

00:03:28.769 --> 00:03:32.560
It'll be interesting to see how the Godzilla:
King of the Monsters movie combines Godzilla,

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:37.900
Rodan and Mothra with King Ghidorah and whether
it'll follow a similar path to the 1964 film

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:41.260
where they're against each other to begin
with, until they decide to combine forces

00:03:41.260 --> 00:03:43.389
to fight the bigger threat of King Ghidorah.

00:03:43.389 --> 00:03:48.620
Kong: Skull Island also sets us up for the
hopefully epic movie Godzilla vs Kong.

00:03:48.620 --> 00:03:52.949
In Skull Island we meet an adolescent Kong
who is already an impressive 100 foot tall,

00:03:52.949 --> 00:03:58.419
but, at this size he would be very hard pressed
to go up against Godzilla who is 350 foot

00:03:58.419 --> 00:03:59.419
tall.

00:03:59.419 --> 00:04:03.510
However, during Skull Island, John C Reilly's
character Marlow mentions that Kong is still

00:04:03.510 --> 00:04:09.689
growing and, as the film is set in 1973, that
means that Kong still has over 40 years of

00:04:09.689 --> 00:04:12.349
growth ahead of him before he meets Godzilla
in 2020.

00:04:12.349 --> 00:04:16.989
By the way, this won't be the first time that
Kong and Godzilla have done battle on the

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:22.900
big screen; that was in the 1962 Japanese
movie King Kong vs Godzilla, which featured

00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:28.130
Kong getting stronger by absorbing electricity
from various sources and appeared to end somewhat

00:04:28.130 --> 00:04:32.620
inconclusively with the pair falling into
the Ocean during a fight after which we only

00:04:32.620 --> 00:04:37.160
see Kong re-emerge though the film's characters
speculated that Godzilla survived.

00:04:37.160 --> 00:04:40.390
Still, according to Toho, Kong was victorious.

00:04:40.390 --> 00:04:45.060
And given that both Godzilla and Kong are
anti-heroes, I imagine that at some point

00:04:45.060 --> 00:04:49.590
they may still put their differences aside,
at least temporarily, to fight a potentially

00:04:49.590 --> 00:04:51.020
even bigger foe.

